WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.482
A builder puts up the same small house twice.

2
00:00:05.482 --> 00:00:08.450
One is a show home that visitors poke around in.

3
00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.232
The other is where a family will really live.

4
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:14.805
The architect copies the drawing for the second plot.

5
00:00:14.805 --> 00:00:16.396
For a while, all is well.

6
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.670
Then a tap in the show home drips.

7
00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.349
The architect fixes the show home's drawing,

8
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:23.485
and forgets the family's copy.

9
00:00:23.485 --> 00:00:29.111
A month later, the family's tap drips too, and nobody can say why the two houses differ.

10
00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:33.299
Copies only stay the same if every change is made twice.

11
00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:34.573
And people forget.

12
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.999
Wouldn't it be nice to keep one drawing, and just fill in a few options per plot?

13
00:00:39.999 --> 00:00:42.378
Builders call that a standard house plan.

14
00:00:42.378 --> 00:00:44.003
Terraform calls it a module.

15
00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:47.145
A module is simply a folder of Terraform files.

16
00:00:47.145 --> 00:00:51.297
You have written modules since the first lesson without knowing it.

17
00:00:51.297 --> 00:00:54.643
The folder where you run terraform is the root module.

18
00:00:54.867 --> 00:01:00.602
When the root uses another folder through a module block, that folder becomes a child module.

19
00:01:00.602 --> 00:01:03.501
Its variables are the options each buyer picks.

20
00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:06.215
Its outputs are what the builder hands back.

21
00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:12.137
In the lab, the infra/local root calls the hello-stack module twice.

22
00:01:12.137 --> 00:01:15.964
Once called dev, with the name hello and port 8080.

23
00:01:16.167 --> 00:01:21.176
And once called test, with the name hello-test and port 8082.

24
00:01:21.176 --> 00:01:22.900
Same plan, two plots.

25
00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:26.952
Every name inside the module is built from the name option.

26
00:01:26.952 --> 00:01:31.936
So Docker gets hello-web and hello-test-web, two containers that never clash.

27
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:36.885
In the state, each call's resources get a module prefix.

28
00:01:36.885 --> 00:01:39.489
module.dev, docker_container.app.

29
00:01:39.489 --> 00:01:42.172
module.test, docker_container.app.

30
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.500
Now the tap drips again, in the show home.

31
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.833
You fix it inside the module, once.

32
00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:50.527
How many stacks change on their next apply?

33
00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:53.518
Both.

34
00:01:53.518 --> 00:01:58.574
Change a module, and every stack that uses it changes on its next apply.

35
00:01:58.574 --> 00:02:02.787
The family's tap gets fixed too, and nobody had to remember.

36
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.543
That is the one way a module differs from a paper plan.

37
00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:09.893
Paper never reaches back into a house already built.

38
00:02:09.893 --> 00:02:12.663
A module does, and that is the whole point.

39
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:20.124
Now a real team has a dev environment and a prod environment, the real one your users depend on.

40
00:02:20.124 --> 00:02:22.536
Remember the state, Terraform's book?

41
00:02:22.536 --> 00:02:24.361
Where does each one keep it?

42
00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:27.581
The tempting shortcut is a workspace.

43
00:02:27.581 --> 00:02:33.529
Say a folder called envs/dev runs one small site, site-dev, on port 8201.

44
00:02:33.529 --> 00:02:40.373
In that folder, terraform workspace new qa gives the same code a second, empty book.

45
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.962
The book is empty, so the plan wants to create everything again.

46
00:02:44.962 --> 00:02:45.985
Read the names.

47
00:02:45.985 --> 00:02:46.871
Will it work?

48
00:02:49.533 --> 00:02:49.771
No.

49
00:02:49.771 --> 00:02:56.182
It plans the same name, site-dev, and the same port, 8201, that dev already uses.

50
00:02:56.182 --> 00:03:01.564
Applying would fail, because the code never uses the workspace name.

51
00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:03.622
And there is a deeper reason.

52
00:03:03.622 --> 00:03:07.270
Workspaces share one backend, the place the book is kept.

53
00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:13.669
HashiCorp's docs call that unsuitable when environments need different credentials and access rules.

54
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.405
The show home's keys should not open the family's front door.

55
00:03:18.633 --> 00:03:24.731
So most teams give each long-lived environment its own folder: envs/dev and envs/prod.

56
00:03:24.731 --> 00:03:31.041
Each is a short root that calls the shared module, with its own backend and its own book.

57
00:03:31.267 --> 00:03:32.558
One plan, shared.

58
00:03:32.558 --> 00:03:34.230
Two books, kept apart.

59
00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.345
An apply in prod has no way to touch dev.

60
00:03:37.567 --> 00:03:43.189
Workspaces still have a use: short-lived copies, such as one stack per feature branch.

61
00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:47.308
Then build names from terraform.workspace, so they never clash.

62
00:03:47.933 --> 00:03:49.169
A few guardrails.

63
00:03:49.169 --> 00:03:55.712
After you add a module block, run terraform init, or plan stops with Module not installed.

64
00:03:55.933 --> 00:04:02.617
Pin every module that comes from elsewhere, because an unpinned one can change your infrastructure on the next init.

65
00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:10.648
Keep modules focused and roots thin: a root sets up providers, calls modules, and connects outputs to inputs.

66
00:04:10.867 --> 00:04:15.519
And since a bug in a shared module now reaches every environment, test it.

67
00:04:15.519 --> 00:04:21.995
terraform test with a mock provider creates nothing real, so it is cheap enough for every pull request.

68
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.271
So the moral of the story.

69
00:04:24.271 --> 00:04:28.191
Write the shape once, as a module, so a fix lands everywhere.

70
00:04:28.191 --> 00:04:32.625
And give each environment its own folder, so each keeps its own book.

71
00:04:33.233 --> 00:04:34.147
A quick check.

72
00:04:34.147 --> 00:04:38.909
Inside the module call named test there is a docker_container called app.

73
00:04:38.909 --> 00:04:41.061
What is its address in the state?

74
00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.074
module.test.docker_container.app.

75
00:04:46.074 --> 00:04:49.815
The call's name goes in front of the normal address.
